# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Match the 42 campus box (captured by tools/env-audit.sh) so `bazel test`
# reflects what the Moulinette actually does.
#
#   box `cc`   -> clang-12      box `gcc` -> gcc-10.5      locale -> en_US.UTF-8
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Bazel-compiled test binaries -- everything the output, valgrind, ASan and
# diff layers build and RUN -- come from //tools/cc_toolchain: campus's exact
# clang 12.0.1-19ubuntu3 and binutils 2.38, fetched against a sha256 and
# registered in MODULE.bazel, so they no longer depend on which box you sat at.
# Every C exercise is ADDITIONALLY compiled under gcc-10 by the per-exercise
# *_compile_gcc tests (see tools/defs.bzl), so both compilers gate one `bazel test`.
#
# The toolchain rules_cc AUTO-DETECTS from the box is never selected by default,
# and never even fetched: `bazel test //...` leaves no local_config_cc in the
# output base, and C 00's :basic passes with no clang-12 on PATH (measured
# 2026-10-03). So the CC it would be detected with is set only by the two
# configs below, and only --config=gcc reaches that toolchain.

# Run tests under the box's locale (the image generates en_US.UTF-8). Locale can
# change collation/ctype, so this keeps local results faithful to the Moulinette.
test --test_env=LANG=en_US.UTF-8
test --test_env=LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8

# Switch the Bazel-compiled binaries' compiler on demand:
#   bazel test //...              -> clang-12, PINNED (//tools/cc_toolchain)
#   bazel test //... --config=gcc -> gcc-10, the BOX's -- not pinned
#
# --config=gcc is an escape hatch, and it is the machine's compiler: it reaches
# the auto-detected toolchain through --extra_toolchains, which outranks the
# registered pinned one. Nothing in the gate uses it; the *_compile_gcc layers
# run the pinned gcc-10 on their own. --config=clang is the default spelled out:
# its CC would name the box's clang-12 only for a toolchain it does not select.
build:clang --repo_env=CC=clang-12
build:gcc --repo_env=CC=gcc-10
build:gcc --extra_toolchains=@local_config_cc_toolchains//:cc-toolchain-k8

# Link the Rust oracle (and rules_rust's internal process_wrapper tool) with bfd
# instead of the toolchain's default gold -- rustc otherwise warns "the gold
# linker is deprecated ... with Rust" on every fresh build. (The ASan C binaries
# get -fuse-ld=bfd via tools/defs.bzl.)
#
# The bare name is safe now, and was not: this comes AFTER the toolchain's own
# link flags, clang takes the last -fuse-ld, and it used to resolve through PATH
# to the box's /usr/bin/ld.bfd whatever the toolchain said. The pinned
# toolchain puts tools/cc_toolchain/ first on clang's program search, so `bfd`
# now finds the pinned linker -- //tools/cc_toolchain:provenance_test checks it.
build --@rules_rust//rust/settings:extra_rustc_flags=-Clink-arg=-fuse-ld=bfd
build --@rules_rust//rust/settings:extra_exec_rustc_flags=-Clink-arg=-fuse-ld=bfd

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A build error fails its own targets; it does not end the run.
#
# Bazel has two keep-going flags. --test_keep_going is on by default, so a
# failing TEST never stops a run. --keep_going (-k) is the one for BUILD
# errors, and it is off by default: the first build action that fails ends the
# invocation, and every target that had not run yet prints NO STATUS.
#
# That first failed action used to be the student's own code: a syntax error,
# a warning under -Werror, a leftover main() or a missing turn-in file was a
# build error, not a red test. Measured on stubs broken that way, without this
# flag: one missing semicolon in C 11 ex00 left 109 of 139 targets NO STATUS, a
# leftover main() in C 01 ex06 left 113 of 117, and a Makefile renamed to
# makefile in Rush 02 ran no test at all. It is not any more -- a student's
# code never fails the build (tools/student_build.sh writes a stand-in that
# fails its tests instead, and a missing file makes them NOT TURNED IN) -- so
# this is now the safety net for a build error of the HARNESS's: a file of its
# own that does not compile, a download that failed.
#
# `build` covers `test` and `run`, which inherit it. Bazel still ends such a
# run with "Build did NOT complete successfully" and exit status 1, and lists
# only the first few FAILED TO BUILD targets: tools/first_red.sh reads all of
# that and puts the exercise that does not build first. `--nokeep_going` on the
# command line brings the stop-at-first-error behaviour back when you want it.
# //tools:conventions keeps this line here.
build --keep_going

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reproducibility: one Bazel version, and a lockfile nothing rewrites behind
# your back.
#
# MODULE.bazel.lock is committed, and its format belongs to the Bazel that
# wrote it — it carries "lockFileVersion": 28. That makes the Bazel version a
# real input to this build rather than a detail of your machine:
#
#   * a NEWER Bazel rewrites the lock into its own format under the default
#     --lockfile_mode=update, silently, during an ordinary `bazel test`. The
#     first symptom is a dirty tree and a ~50 KB diff nobody asked for.
#   * an OLDER Bazel cannot use the lock and re-resolves every module against
#     the BCR, which needs network. A campus box may not reach it, and the
#     failure surfaces as an unrelated-looking fetch error mid-build.
#
# So the version is pinned in //.bazelversion, which bazelisk reads on every
# invocation and downloads on demand. That file holds the bare version string
# and NOTHING else: bazelisk does not accept comments there — it tries to
# resolve a leading "#..." line as a version number and dies — which is why
# this explanation lives here.
#
# --lockfile_mode=error is the belt to that brace. .bazelversion only binds
# someone who goes through bazelisk; a distro-packaged `bazel` on PATH, or
# USE_BAZEL_VERSION in the environment, walks straight past it. With `error` a
# lock that does not match stops the build and says so, instead of quietly
# rewriting a committed file.
#
# The cost is that a deliberate MODULE.bazel edit now fails until you refresh
# the lock on purpose. Do that by re-running the command you were already
# running with the mode overridden on the command line, which beats this entry:
#
#     bazel test //... --lockfile_mode=update   # then commit MODULE.bazel.lock
#
# That extra step IS the feature: the lock moves in a reviewable commit of its
# own, not as a side effect of someone running the test suite.
#
# Do NOT reach for `bazel mod deps` to refresh it, which is the obvious guess.
# Unlike a build, it evaluates EVERY module extension eagerly — including two
# this repo never uses, both of them declared by our deps rather than by
# //MODULE.bazel: rules_rust's crate_universe `cu_nr` (the oracle crate has zero
# dependencies, so no crates.io repo is ever fetched) and pybind11_bazel's
# `internal_configure`. Neither is in the committed lock. So under `error`
# `bazel mod deps` exits 2 saying they "do not exist in the lockfile", which
# reads as a broken pin when nothing is wrong; and under `update` it would write
# both into the lock as churn unrelated to whatever you actually changed.
# The build itself is unaffected — measured on 9.2.0, `bazel query //...`
# resolves all 2016 targets clean under `error`, because the loading phase only
# touches the extensions the build genuinely needs.
common --lockfile_mode=error

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE BAZEL'S STATE GOES -- deliberately not set in this file.
#
# A full run of this repo builds ~1300 test binaries plus 84 ASan twins,
# measured at 8.9 GB under the output root, and a 42 machine caps $HOME at about
# 5 GB. So the root has to be somewhere with room, and it has to be PER USER:
# two students on one machine sharing a root collide on its lock, or the second
# cannot write the first one's directory at all.
#
# An rc file cannot say "per user": it expands %workspace% and nothing else, no
# $USER, no $HOME. This file used to carry `startup
# --output_user_root=/tmp/bazelcache` as the fallback for every checkout without
# a .bazelrc.local -- one shared path, on exactly the machines it was written
# for (finding 001). So the root now comes from, in order:
#
#   1. --output_user_root on the command line;
#   2. .bazelrc.local (imported at the BOTTOM of this file, see there), which
#      `sh tools/setup.sh` writes for THIS checkout -- every clone and every
#      worktree has its own, so run setup.sh in each; or ~/.bazelrc;
#   3. otherwise tools/bazel, the wrapper bazelisk runs on every `bazel`
#      command, which reads $USER and adds the root setup.sh would have chosen
#      (tools/drives.sh): /goinfre/$USER/bazel on campus,
#      ~/.cache/42-piscine/bazel elsewhere. Off campus, a /tmp/bazelcache that
#      is already yours is kept, so that this change relocates nobody's state.
#
# `bazel info output_base` says which applied: the root is the folder above
# it. (There is no `bazel info output_user_root`.) See docs/environment.md,
# "Where the state goes".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INODES, which run out long before bytes do.
#
# On 2026-08-14 every tool on the dev box failed with "ENOSPC: no space left on
# device" while `df -h` showed 19% used: `df -i` was 16,777,216 of 16,777,216.
# The filesystem has a fixed number of files it can ever hold, and this suite
# is made of files it does not need to keep.
#
# MEASURED, one output base after a full `bazel test //...`: 2,101,054 inodes,
# of which 2,079,111 -- 99.5% -- were RUNFILES TREES: a symlink forest per test,
# mirroring every data file it names. The ilp32 layer alone was 1,574,152 of
# them, 96 trees of 16,397 entries each, because every one stages zig's entire
# SDK. Bytes, almost none: they are symlinks.
#
# A sandboxed test does not read those trees. It gets its inputs laid out in
# its own sandbox, per run, which Bazel reuses and trims. The trees in bazel-out
# are for the things that DO run in place -- `bazel run`, and tests forced local
# like shell-01 ex04 -- and with this flag Bazel builds a tree only when one of
# those asks for it, rather than for every test it ever built.
build --nobuild_runfile_links

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No empty __init__.py made in a Python target's runfiles.
#
# rules_python otherwise makes one in every folder of a py_binary's runfiles,
# and says so on every analysis in a DEBUG/WARNING banner ("Target
# @@//tools:norminette is using implicit __init__.py creation") that a student
# reads as an error (finding 047). Repo-wide, so the next py_binary or py_test
# a project adds does not bring it back; nothing here imports through one
# (norminette_main.py runs as a script, and the pip package carries its own).
# //tools:conventions checks the line is here while any BUILD file has a
# Python target.
common --incompatible_default_to_explicit_init_py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leaving the machine usable while the suite runs
#
# A campus box is a 6-core i5-8500 with 8 GB of RAM, ~2.5 GB free at idle. Left
# to itself Bazel takes all six cores, and its server settles around 1.4 GB of
# RSS on this repo. Against 2.5 GB free that is what turns a slow machine into a
# frozen one: the shortage is memory first and CPU second, so the flags here
# help but the habits at the bottom of this section matter more.
#
# Both limits below are written in Bazel's portable form, so the same line does
# the right thing on a 6-core campus box (leaves 2 free) and on a 24-core dev
# container (leaves 2 free). There is nothing to change per machine.
#
#   cpu             how much CPU Bazel schedules against, for builds and tests
#   local_test_jobs how many TESTS run at once. Capped separately because this
#                   suite's tests spawn their own heavy children — valgrind,
#                   callgrind, zig, ASan binaries — and Bazel budgets a test at
#                   one CPU regardless, so the cpu figure alone under-counts.
build --local_resources=cpu=HOST_CPUS-2
test --local_test_jobs=HOST_CPUS-2

# A heap cap on the Bazel server. Worth having, but do not over-credit it:
# -Xmx bounds the JAVA HEAP, not the process. Measured here, peak server RSS
# over a full `bazel test //...` was 1431 MB at -Xmx1024m against 1677 MB
# uncapped — a real ~250 MB, not the order of magnitude it first looks like,
# because metaspace, code cache, thread stacks and native allocations sit
# outside the cap. On the campus box the JVM default is ~25% of 8 GB, so the
# saving there is smaller still.
#
# Verified: the full suite under this cap gives the identical 1321 pass /
# 114 fail as an unrestricted run, in 117s, with no OutOfMemory and nothing
# timing out from the reduced parallelism. Analysing all 2016 targets fits in
# 195 MB, so 1 GB is ample headroom; raise it only if analysis ever OOMs.
#
# Changing a `startup` flag restarts the server, so the next command is slower.
startup --host_jvm_args=-Xmx1024m

# WHAT ACTUALLY KEEPS THE BOX ALIVE, in order of effect — the flags above are
# the smaller half of it:
#
#   1. Do not run //... on a 2.5 GB-free machine. The server alone settles
#      around 1.4 GB whatever you cap it at. Use the per-level suites while you
#      work; they are what the level system exists for:
#          bazel test //c-piscine/c-piscine-c-05:basic     what the Moulinette grades
#      and :strict, :robust or :complete when you want more (what each adds:
#      docs/reference.md). See docs/testing.md, "Working on one thing at a time".
#
#   2. `bazel shutdown` when you stop working. The server stays up after every
#      command, holding that 1.4 GB, and by default leaves only once it has
#      been idle for three hours (--max_idle_secs, 10800 s: `bazel help
#      startup_options`).
#
#   3. Watch for ORPHANED servers. Each output root gets its own server, so
#      changing --output_user_root (as this file did) leaves the previous one
#      running and holding its memory. Measured on this machine right after
#      that change: 3.5 GB across two servers — on an 8 GB box, most of it.
#
#      List them, with the root each one belongs to:
#          for p in $(pgrep -f 'bazel\(42\)'); do \
#            printf '%6s MB  %s\n' "$(($(ps -o rss= -p $p)/1024))" \
#              "$(tr '\0' '\n' </proc/$p/cmdline | grep -m1 logging.config.file)"; \
#          done
#
#      Then kill the one you no longer use, BY PID. `bazel
#      --output_user_root=<old path> shutdown` looks like the right answer and
#      does nothing — tried both with and without --ignore_all_rc_files, the
#      orphan survived both; a plain `kill <pid>` reclaimed 2453 MB at once.
#          kill <pid>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-checkout overrides. LAST, and that is load-bearing.
#
# Bazel takes the LAST value it reads for a startup option, so a file imported
# at the TOP cannot override anything set below it. This import once sat above
# a `startup --output_user_root=/tmp/bazelcache` line, which meant
# .bazelrc.local could not move the output root -- the one thing it exists for,
# and the one thing tools/setup.sh writes into it. setup.sh reported
# "output_user_root -> /goinfre/you/bazel" while Bazel kept using the shared
# path, and nothing said so, because the message was printed by the script
# rather than read back from Bazel. That line is gone (see "WHERE BAZEL'S STATE
# GOES" above), but the host_jvm_args startup line is still above, and a later
# one may join it.
#
# Imported last, this file does what its name says: this checkout's settings
# win. It is gitignored and sits in the checkout, so every clone and worktree
# has its own (tools/setup.sh writes it).
try-import %workspace%/.bazelrc.local
